{"reply":"In Chapters 2 and 5 of *Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland*, Alice’s changing size makes her identity feel uncertain, while that uncertainty makes each physical change more disturbing. In Chapter 2, she treats bodily difference as evidence that she may no longer be herself: “if I’m not the same, the next question is, Who in the world am I?” (Chapter 2 — *The Pool of Tears*). Her mistaken schoolwork and altered voice deepen the sense that identity includes memory, knowledge, and language—not just appearance.

Chapter 5 makes the connection explicit. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice answers that she has “been changed several times,” then links that change to her inability to explain herself: “I can’t explain _myself_ … because I’m not myself, you see.” She immediately describes being “so many different sizes in a day” as confusing (Chapter 5 — *Advice from a Caterpillar*).

So the relationship works in both directions: changing size disrupts Alice’s physical continuity, and that disruption makes her question whether the person inside the changing body is still the same Alice. The novel turns identity into something unstable and difficult to define rather than something guaranteed by appearance. Which seems more unsettling for Alice here: not knowing what she looks like, or not knowing what still makes her “herself”?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch02-ln0327-0360","source_location":"Chapter 2 — The Pool of Tears, source lines 327-360","exact_quote":"“if I’m not the same, the next question is, Who in the world am I?”","supported_claims":["In Chapter 2, she treats bodily difference as evidence that she may no longer be herself: “if I’m not the same, the next question is, Who in the world am I?” (Chapter 2 — *The Pool of Tears*).","Her mistaken schoolwork and altered voice deepen the sense that identity includes memory, knowledge, and language—not just appearance."]},{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","exact_quote":"“Who are _you?_”","supported_claims":["Chapter 5 makes the connection explicit. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice answers that she has “been changed several times,” then links that change to her inability to explain herself: “I can’t explain _myself_ … because I’m not myself, you see.” She immediately describes being “so many different sizes in a day” as confusing (Chapter 5 — *Advice from a Caterpillar*).","So the relationship works in both directions: changing size disrupts Alice’s physical continuity, and that disruption makes her question whether the person inside the changing body is still the same Alice.","The novel turns identity into something unstable and difficult to define rather than something guaranteed by appearance."]}],"memory":{